# Descriptor for this agent. Deliberately empty of declarations.
#
# agent-mgr derives everything from the REGISTRY NAME, and for `property` every
# default is already what this agent runs: AGENT_HOME ~/.hermes-property,
# AGENT_CONTAINER hermes-property, AGENT_PROJECT hermes-property, and
# AGENT_CONFIG config.yaml beside this file.
#
# Declaring any of them would pin this repo to one person. A repo that declares
# AGENT_HOME cannot be shared at all -- agent-mgr's require_own_home refuses the
# second instance, because it resolves to the first one's home. Leaving the
# descriptor bare is what lets a second person register their own row against
# their own checkout and get their own home, container and project.
#
# Per-person values that are NOT identity -- a different Mac, say -- belong in
# ~/.hermes-<name>/.env and reach config.yaml as ${VAR}, which the gateway
# interpolates at runtime. mcp_servers.latch below already works that way. The
# model is NOT one of them: both the primary and the compression fallback are
# literals in config.yaml, so a model in a person's dotenv reaches nothing.
# AGENT_TZ is the exception, because Compose renders TZ before the gateway
# ever reads the file.
#
# The one declaration below is not identity. Instances of this repo are live:
# real people's workflows run through them, and the gateway messages its
# person at every restart -- so agent-mgr asks [y/N] before any transition,
# and refuses non-interactively unless AGENT_TRANSITION_ACK=1 says the
# restart is the point.
AGENT_LIVE=1
#
# The skill is seeded into the agent's writable home by deploy-hook -- copied
# when absent or empty, never over a non-empty agent-owned copy. A hook
# path is not identity: it resolves against each instance's own checkout.
AGENT_DEPLOY_HOOK=deploy-hook
